before:
```typescript
async function makeGreeting(name: string): Promise<string> {
  return "Hello, " + name;
}
async function showGreeting(): Promise<void> {
  const text = await makeGreeting("Nora");
  console.log(text);
}
const demonstration = showGreeting();
```
This prints Hello, Nora. makeGreeting takes name:string and returns Promise<string>; its body returns the greeting, which async wraps as a promise. showGreeting has no inputs. await pauses that function until makeGreeting supplies text, then console.log displays it. Promise<void> means showGreeting has no useful fulfilled value. showGreeting() starts this local demonstration; const demonstration keeps its returned promise so a caller can know when the display is finished. It does not make the operation synchronous. No server is involved.

